<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    OWNER: shared, maintained with tools/ by Dev D.

    WHY IT EXISTS

    Nothing in this repository compiles Unity code. .github/workflows/ci.yml says so in its
    own comment: a licensed Editor is needed on the runner and the free tier does not
    provide one, so the unity-compile job is commented out. Everything under
    Ironfront_Reborn/Assets/Scripts/ is therefore edited on trust and only found to be
    broken when Dev A next opens the Editor.

    This closes the cheapest part of that hole. It parses the files with Roslyn at the
    language version Unity actually uses, and fails on a syntax error. That catches an
    unbalanced brace, a mistyped expression, and — the reason for the version pin — C# 10+
    syntax that a .NET 8 developer writes without thinking and Unity 6000.3 rejects.

    WHAT IT DOES NOT DO

    It does not resolve types, so it will not notice a missing using, a misspelled member,
    or a wrong argument type. It is a syntax gate, not a compiler. Read a green run as
    "this will parse", never as "this will build".
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>Ironfront.Tools.UnitySyntaxCheck</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>UnitySyntaxCheck</AssemblyName>
    <Description>Parses Unity scripts at Unity's C# language version and fails on syntax errors.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- No Version: Central Package Management (Directory.Packages.props) owns it. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
